v1.0.0-rc.4 be12ebd126fdf03dcea9eade7bcec3afbcba001b
#
# ENGINE_PIN — the vouchfx engine version and commit this MCP server is pinned to.
#
# What it pins
# ------------
# The first line is  <version> <full-40-character-commit-sha>  — two
# space-separated fields, e.g. "v1.0.0-alpha.9 8c579ab4315cacba4066bc3f33dc24a19ca6c3d1".
# src/Vouchfx.Mcp/EnginePin.cs parses exactly this format.
#
# Unlike vouchfx-samples (which shallow-fetches and builds the engine from
# source at a pinned commit), vouchfx-mcp wraps the PUBLISHED `vouchfx` dotnet
# tool as a subprocess — it never builds the engine from source. The two
# fields therefore pin two different things:
#
#   - VERSION  is what the CLI handshake verifies against at run time: the MCP
#     server invokes the installed `vouchfx` tool, reads its reported version,
#     and refuses to proceed (or warns) if it does not match this line. This
#     is the field that actually gates behaviour.
#
#   - COMMIT SHA is the engine ref that the vendored artefacts this repo ships
#     — the JSON Schema, the generated language reference, and the recipe
#     library (vendored under vendored/ and drift-gated in CI) — are drift-gated against. CI
#     re-generates or diffs those artefacts against the engine source at this
#     exact commit so a silent drift between "what the docs/schema say" and
#     "what the pinned CLI version actually does" is caught mechanically
#     rather than discovered by a user.
#
# A full SHA is required, not a short SHA or a branch/tag name, for the same
# reproducibility reason as every other fleet satellite's ENGINE_PIN: a moving
# ref defeats the point of a pin.
#
# If the SHA above is ever the all-zeros placeholder, EnginePin.Load/Parse
# will refuse to run against it (see EnginePinTests.cs).
#
# When to advance it
# -------------------
# Bump this file when:
#   - a new `vouchfx` pre-release/release is published on NuGet.org that this
#     MCP server should target (most common reason — this repo tracks
#     published tool versions, not engine milestones directly);
#   - the vendored JSON Schema / language reference / recipes need to move to
#     a newer engine commit (e.g. a schema field or step family was added);
#   - the engine's v1.x SDK/event contract freeze advances and this server's
#     event-stream parsing should validate against the new baseline.
#
# How to advance it
# ------------------
# Two easy ways to find the commit SHA for a given tag/release WITHOUT cloning:
#   - GitHub: open the release/tag page (e.g.
#     github.com/tomas-rampas/vouchfx/releases/tag/v1.0.0-alpha.9) and click
#     the short-SHA link under the tag name to reach the full SHA.
#   - `gh api repos/tomas-rampas/vouchfx/commits/<tag> --jq .sha` — PREFER
#     THIS. It resolves straight to the commit SHA for either kind of tag,
#     with no peeling to get wrong.
#   - `git ls-remote --tags https://github.com/tomas-rampas/vouchfx '<tag>*'`
#     — GOTCHA, corrected 2026-08-10 after measuring the real refs. Two
#     things the previous wording got wrong:
#       (a) vouchfx's tags are MIXED, not uniformly annotated. Measured:
#           v1.0.0-rc.1 and v1.0.0-rc.4 are ANNOTATED (tag object != commit),
#           v1.0.0-rc.3 is LIGHTWEIGHT (the ref IS the commit). So you cannot
#           assume either shape — check, do not extrapolate.
#       (b) the bare `<tag>` pattern prints only ONE line even for an
#           annotated tag, because the peeled ref is literally named
#           `refs/tags/<tag>^{}` and does not match the pattern `<tag>`.
#           Following the old recipe verbatim on v1.0.0-rc.4 yields
#           9775354b… — the TAG OBJECT's SHA, i.e. exactly the wrong value.
#           Use the trailing-glob form above so the `^{}` line is emitted too.
#     Rule regardless of shape: ENGINE_PIN holds the COMMIT SHA. If a `^{}`
#     line is present, that is the one; if the tag is lightweight, the single
#     line already is the commit.
#     Cross-check the result for free: the installed CLI's informational
#     version embeds the engine commit — `vouchfx --version` on the pinned
#     build prints `<version>+<commit sha>`, which must equal this file's
#     first line field-for-field.
#
# Steps:
#   1. Pick the new `vouchfx` version to target and its corresponding engine
#      commit SHA (peeled, per the gotcha above).
#   2. Replace the first line of this file with the new "<version> <sha>" pair.
#   3. Re-validate: re-run the packaged-CLI handshake and the vendored
#      artefact drift check (see vendored/README.md) against the new pin.
#   4. Run the graceful-teardown LIVE DRILL against the newly-pinned CLI and
#      CONFIRM IT PASSES before proceeding (see docs/validation/graceful-teardown-drill.md
#      for the exact procedure). This is MANDATORY: the MCP's GracefulShutdownGrace (35s)
#      is only safe while the engine's own teardown budget stays ≤ ~30s. The drill is
#      the only gate that catches a budget divergence — if a future engine release grows
#      its teardown budget past 35s, the drill will fail empirically (engine self-exits
#      after the grace deadline, MCP force-kills, topology is orphaned) rather than
#      silently regressing to the old orphan-then-Ryuk-reaps behaviour.
#   5. Open a PR with the pin bump (plus any artefact regeneration the new version
#      requires) so the change is reviewable in isolation. Evidence of the passed drill
#      (e.g., a note in the PR description referencing the drill procedure and confirming
#      it passed) is recommended for transparency.
#
# Pin history
# ------------
# 2026-08-10: advanced to v1.0.0-rc.4 (be12ebd1) — authenticated infrastructure: a `security:`
# block on services and kafka dependencies, services generalised beyond HTTP (raw TCP `ports`,
# author-declared `healthCheck`, compose-style host-port pinning "19093:9093"), HTTPS with client
# certificates, mutual TLS for both Kafka providers, and a fail-closed pre-run confirmation probe
# reporting one of two named levels (an unconfirmable `security:` declaration exits non-zero).
# Vendored artefacts regenerated at this commit with `pwsh ./scripts/sync-vendored.ps1 -Update`:
# composed-schema.v1.json (65,154 -> 156,470 bytes; $defs 3 -> 10, adding security,
# securityServerArtifact, service, serviceHealthCheck, dependency, seedDependency, captureEntry —
# purely additive) and language-reference.md (+67/-25 lines: new `security`/`ports`/`healthCheck`
# surfaces and corrected `description` text). recipes.md was byte-identical and did not move.
#
# The schema changes altered this server's own behaviour. validate_suite re-derives the engine's
# error collection against the same schema rather than wrapping it, so a schema change alone moves
# its output — which is what happened here, and is handled in the same PR:
#   - $defs/step closed: "additionalProperties": true -> "unevaluatedProperties": false. This is
#     what finally makes a typo'd step field an error, and it brings the well-known annotation-
#     withholding cascade with it. Measured: one http.rest step missing `target` produced FOUR
#     errors from validate_suite versus the engine CLI's ONE, three of them naming VALID fields.
#   - Composites went from 2 to 9 ($defs/service's image|project choice, four db-assert `expect`
#     anyOfs, both azureservicebus providers). A composite reports every LOSING branch even when a
#     sibling wins, and those phantoms then counted as a step's "other error" and made the cascade
#     hide the real one. Measured: a well-formed mq-expect.azureservicebus step with a typo
#     reported TWO false errors and NOT the typo; every suite declaring a service was told to add
#     `project` alongside `image`, which the schema itself then forbids — advice that loops.
#   - $defs/security gained the closure AND allOf/if clauses, which exposed three pre-existing
#     filter defects: `if`-discriminator nodes (never shown to anyone) were deleting real errors;
#     roll-up suppression was decided per NODE, so a node carrying both a genuine `required` failure
#     and an aggregate lost the genuine one; and the specificity test was not scoped to the
#     keyword's own subschema, so a failure under one keyword deleted a DIFFERENT keyword's finding
#     on the same node. The last one bit hardest on a step matching two branches of its `oneOf`:
#     the `oneOf` finding was deleted, which disarmed the cascade, and mq-expect.azureservicebus
#     then reported five REQUIRED fields — `target` among them — as unknown properties, against the
#     CLI's single correct finding.
#   - Errors located inside a block that a `properties/<name>: false` clause had already refused
#     outright were still reported. A `security` block on a redis dependency cannot exist at all,
#     yet the author was told to add `endpoint` to it. (Ported from the engine's
#     SuppressErrorsInsideForbiddenContainer, itself new in this very release.)
#   - Error volume now tracks the document's key count rather than the schema's shape, which made
#     three pre-existing quadratic costs reachable: a full YAML re-parse per error on the schema
#     path, the same on the unknown-step-type path, and a pointer re-stringification per roll-up
#     comparison. Measured against the validation worker's 10s budget: a 33 KB suite took 14.4s, a
#     1 000-step suite 135.5s, and a 2 000-unknown-type suite 31.9s. Now 0.8s, 3.1s and 2.5s.
#   - Every closed object's rejection reached the author as "[] All values fail against the false
#     schema" — an empty keyword tag naming nothing.
#   - Every one of the 25 step types gained a type-level description (9 of 25 had one under rc.3).
#
# Every one of those is fixed here. What is NOT claimed, because it was measured and is not true:
# that validate_suite and `vouchfx validate` now produce identical text everywhere. The honest
# figure comes from running the ENGINE'S OWN rejected corpus — all 55 fixtures at this pinned commit
# (tests/Vouchfx.Engine.Compilation.Tests/Corpus/Rejected) — through both binaries:
#
#     33 byte-identical | 13 same findings, less enriched wording | 0 set-differences | 9 not
#     comparable (the CLI short-circuits at [Parse]/[Pipeline] before schema validation)
#
# Baseline before this work, same corpus, same command: 19 identical, 27 divergent. The number that
# matters is the third: across all 46 comparable fixtures validate_suite reports no extra, missing,
# or relocated finding relative to the CLI. Read that as scoped to this corpus, not as a universal
# claim — every fixture in it pins a SINGLE defect, so it cannot by construction exercise a rule
# about how two findings interact. Those rules (the composite, forbidden-container and
# unevaluatedProperties suppressions) are covered instead by purpose-built interaction fixtures in
# RealValidateAgainstPinnedCliTests, because a clean corpus run does not reach them. The 13 carry the
# same findings at the same locations with less enriched text, because the engine derives that text
# from per-clause formatters (FormatEnumError, FormatConstError, FormatForbiddenPropertyError) whose
# wording is hand-authored per schema clause and carries release-position prose. Copying those would
# create a second copy rotting independently of the engine — the exact failure this pin demonstrates.
# Both the agreement and the deliberate wording gap are asserted by RealValidateAgainstPinnedCliTests
# against the installed pinned binary.
#
# CAVEAT on that assertion, stated because a green CI run is easy to misread: those tests SKIP when
# no installed CLI matches this pin, and CI installs none (vouchfx-mcp#40 tracks fixing that). A
# green CI run is therefore NOT evidence the oracle ran — it runs on a maintainer's machine, which
# is where pin bumps are performed.
#
# STRUCTURAL NOTE for whoever advances this pin next: validate_suite re-derives the engine's error
# collection rather than consuming it, so every pin re-opens this gap. This one ported 8 of the
# engine's collection mechanisms; what remains unported is the enrichment formatters (enum, const,
# and the per-clause forbidden-property prose), which change WORDING only. That "wording only"
# split is itself a measured claim and was wrong once already: the forbidden-CONTAINER subsumption
# looked like an enrichment gap and was actually a set-changing collection pass, found only by
# constructing a two-defect document the corpus does not contain. Either the engine grows a
# consumable package for this, or validate_suite stays authoritative for VALIDITY and best-effort
# for WORDING (docs/tools-and-resources.md says the latter). Do not assume the next schema change
# is cosmetic — run the corpus AND write an interaction fixture for anything new.
#
# 2026-08-10: graceful-teardown LIVE DRILL run against this rc.4 pin (installed CLI reporting
# 1.0.0-rc.4+be12ebd126fdf03dcea9eade7bcec3afbcba001b — version AND embedded commit both matching
# this file's first line), with TESTCONTAINERS_RYUK_DISABLED=true. Zero Ryuk containers existed at
# any point in any run (checked explicitly, not assumed), so only the engine's own teardown can
# account for the cleanup below. Teardown is measured from the stdin close (the graceful-stop
# signal) to the child process's own exit:
#   - Abort mid-topology-stand-up (stdin closed at a fixed t=20s, three runs): 10.12s / 4.57s /
#     5.74s. The spread is the fixed delay landing at different points of stand-up, not teardown
#     variance. Worst case 10.12s against the 35s GracefulShutdownGrace — 24.9s of headroom.
#   - Abort after the topology was fully stood up (the heavier upper bound, two runs): 5.79s and
#     5.80s, both ending in the correct `Scenario ... INCONCLUSIVE (inconclusive=1)` verdict.
#     29.2s of headroom against the 35s grace.
# Containers and the aspire-session-network-* network were gone 0.22-1.05s after the child exited
# in four of five runs; in the fifth (the 10.12s run) the broker container and the network outlived
# the child briefly and were gone by the settle check. Post-run Docker state matched the pre-run
# baseline exactly in every run: zero suite containers, zero aspire-session-network-*, zero
# volumes created that day.
#
# The rc.4 teardown budget did NOT grow. Measured like-for-like on the same host, same day, same
# readiness trigger, by downgrading the installed CLI to rc.3 and re-running the heavier scenario:
# rc.3 5.75s vs rc.4 5.79s — a 0.04s difference, i.e. noise. The new pre-run confirmation probe
# opens TLS connections per secured target and was the specific risk to this budget; note the scope
# limit of that result, which is why it is written down rather than generalised: the drill suite
# (samples/orders-dotnet) declares NO `security:` block, so the probe had no secured target to
# confirm and this drill does not measure its cost. A suite that DOES declare `security:` should be
# drilled separately before that cost is assumed to be zero.
#
# Exit codes observed during this drill. These are TWO different things and an earlier draft of this
# entry wrongly called both a defect — corrected here, because asserting a defect against a
# documented invariant is exactly the kind of claim this file has already had to retract once:
#   - Cancelled AFTER execution began: prints `Scenario ... INCONCLUSIVE (inconclusive=1)`, exits 0.
#     This is BY DESIGN, not a defect. The engine's hard invariant (blueprint §12.1) is "only Fail
#     breaks CI by default", and an execution-time Inconclusive exiting 0 is that taxonomy working.
#     vouchfx#369 explicitly keeps that case opt-in-gated.
#   - Cancelled MID-STAND-UP: prints `topology failed to start`, and also exits 0. This one is
#     genuinely suspect — a topology that never started reads as an Environment error, which the
#     taxonomy places at exit 3, not 0. Tracked upstream as vouchfx#369 ("a suite that parsed but
#     never executed exits 0 by default"), open since 2026-08-04.
# Both were measured identically on rc.3, so neither is an rc.4 regression and neither blocks this
# pin. docs/validation/graceful-teardown-drill.md previously told drill-runners to expect exit code
# 4 for these and has been corrected.
#
# vouchfx-mcp#42 ("GracefulShutdownGrace (35s) has only ~5s headroom over the engine's heavier
# teardown path") is REFUTED by the measurements above and should be closed or rewritten: the
# heavier path measured 5.79s and 5.80s against the 35s grace (29.2s of headroom), and the rc.3
# control run at 5.75s shows the 30.05s figure that issue rests on is not reproducible on this host.
#
# NOT reproducible, and recorded so the next maintainer does not chase it: the rc.3 entry below
# reports 30.05s for the heavier scenario. Re-running that same rc.3 build on this host today
# measured 5.75s. The whole orders suite now completes in ~31s wall, so the 40s+ delay that entry's
# procedure suggests would land after the run had already finished. Most likely the 2026-07-30
# figure came from a cold-cache run with image pulls in the stand-up path. The rc.3-vs-rc.4 pair
# above was therefore measured fresh rather than compared against that number.
